<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mikvé Israel-Emanuel Synagogue</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Snoa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mikvé Israel-Emanuel Synagogue in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/willemstad">Willemstad</a>, Curaçao, houses the oldest Jewish congregation in continuous existence in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sephardic-jews">Sephardic Jews</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> in the 1650s. The present building, known as the Snoa, was dedicated in 1732 and is the oldest synagogue in continuous use in the Americas. Its floor is covered with sand, a tradition shared with a few other Sephardic synagogues of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mikve-israel-emanuel-synagogue.jpg" alt="Mikvé Israel-Emanuel Synagogue" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dolly442 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cura%C3%A7ao_synagogue1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
